BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial misstatement threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is a ''clearly trivial'' misstatement under AU-C 450?', '["A misstatement below the overall materiality level set for the audit", "A misstatement that is less than performance materiality but greater than zero", "A misstatement that is inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate", "A misstatement that management agrees is not material to users"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, ''clearly trivial'' describes misstatements that are inconsequential, whether taken individually or in the aggregate, considering size, nature, and circumstances. This threshold is set well below materiality. Unlike choice A, clearly trivial is not simply below overall materiality—it represents an amount so small that the auditor need not accumulate it for evaluation purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Projected misstatement plus sampling risk allowance
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redwood Manufacturing, the auditor selected a sample of 60 inventory items from a population valued at $4.2 million. The sample revealed pricing errors totaling $12,600, and the tolerable misstatement was set at $84,000. After projecting the sample results to the population, the auditor should:', '["Accept the population because the sample errors are less than tolerable misstatement", "Consider whether the projected misstatement plus an allowance for sampling risk exceeds tolerable misstatement", "Reject the population because any sample error automatically indicates material misstatement", "Request management adjust all 60 sampled items before concluding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 530.A20–A22, the auditor projects sample misstatements to the population and adds an allowance for sampling risk when evaluating results. The total projected misstatement plus the sampling risk allowance is compared to tolerable misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because it compares only the raw sample error amount rather than the projected misstatement with its sampling risk allowance applied to the full population.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Purpose of the schedule of uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the purpose of the schedule of uncorrected misstatements that auditors accumulate during an audit?', '["To document all identified misstatements for evaluating their aggregate effect on the financial statements", "To provide management with a binding list of required adjustments before issuing the report", "To calculate the exact amount of total error in the financial statements", "To serve as evidence that the auditor detected every misstatement in the records"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor accumulates identified misstatements during the audit so their aggregate effect can be evaluated near audit completion. This schedule supports the auditor''s assessment of whether uncorrected misstatements are material. Choice B is incorrect because the schedule does not bind management to make corrections—management may decline to adjust, and the auditor then evaluates the impact on the opinion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Iron curtain vs rollover approach for carryover misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Pinecrest Electronics, the auditor identifies a $40,000 overstatement of depreciation expense that originated two years ago and remains uncorrected. The cumulative balance sheet effect is $80,000, while the current-year income statement effect is $0. Overall materiality is $100,000. When evaluating this carryover misstatement, the auditor should:', '["Disregard the misstatement because it has no current-year income statement impact", "Report only the $40,000 originating amount as the relevant misstatement", "Require management to restate prior-year financial statements before issuing the report", "Consider both the cumulative balance sheet effect and the current-year income statement effect"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A7–A8, the auditor evaluates uncorrected misstatements using both the income statement (rollover) approach and the balance sheet (iron curtain) approach. The rollover method considers the current-year effect ($0), while the iron curtain method considers the cumulative effect ($80,000). Choice A is incorrect because ignoring the cumulative balance sheet impact could overlook a material misstatement of financial position.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors making quantitatively immaterial misstatement significant
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Maple Ridge Pharmaceuticals identifies an uncorrected $18,000 misstatement in officer compensation disclosures. Overall materiality is $500,000, making the amount quantitatively immaterial. However, the auditor believes the misstatement was intentional. In this situation, the auditor should:', '["Document the misstatement as clearly trivial and take no further action", "Evaluate the qualitative characteristics of the misstatement, including the possibility of fraud", "Automatically issue a qualified opinion for any intentional misstatement", "Increase overall materiality to ensure the misstatement remains immaterial"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in aggregate, considering both quantitative and qualitative factors. An intentional misstatement—even if quantitatively small—may indicate fraud risk that affects the overall evaluation. Choice A is incorrect because a potentially intentional misstatement cannot be dismissed as clearly trivial regardless of its dollar amount.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Management representation about uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the engagement wrap-up for Birchfield Retail, the auditor prepares the management representation letter. Regarding uncorrected misstatements, AU-C 450 requires the auditor to request that management include a representation that:', '["All identified misstatements have been corrected in the financial statements", "Management is not responsible for misstatements below the materiality threshold", "Management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "The auditor assumes full responsibility for any undetected misstatements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor requests written representations from management that it believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of such items is attached to the letter. Choice A is incorrect because uncorrected misstatements by definition remain unadjusted—the representation addresses their perceived immateriality, not their correction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Aggregating projected misstatements across accounts in the same direction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Granite Peak Construction, the auditor identifies projected misstatements of $35,000 in revenue, $22,000 in accounts receivable, and $18,000 in cost of goods sold, all overstating net income. Overall materiality is $90,000. When evaluating the aggregate effect, the auditor should most appropriately:', '["Combine the projected misstatements because they affect net income in the same direction and compare the total to materiality", "Evaluate each misstatement independently against materiality since they arise from different accounts", "Offset the revenue overstatement against the cost of goods sold overstatement", "Disregard the accounts receivable misstatement because it only affects the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.10–.11, the auditor evaluates the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements, considering their combined effect on the financial statements. When misstatements move net income in the same direction, they are additive ($75,000 total). Choice B is incorrect because evaluating each misstatement independently ignores the cumulative impact that could approach or exceed materiality when combined.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Control deficiency in design defined
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is a control deficiency in design as distinguished from a control deficiency in operation?', '["A control that was not performed consistently by qualified personnel", "A control that operated effectively but was not documented properly", "A control that detected an error after the transaction was processed", "A control that is missing or not properly designed to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a design deficiency exists when a necessary control is missing or when an existing control is not properly designed to achieve its objective of preventing or detecting misstatements on a timely basis. Choice A describes an operating deficiency, where a properly designed control does not operate as intended or is performed by someone lacking the authority or competence to do so.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Pervasive misstatement effects defined
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What does ''pervasive'' mean when describing the effects of misstatements on financial statements?', '["The misstatement exceeds overall materiality for the audit engagement", "The effects are not confined to specific elements but affect multiple areas, represent a substantial portion, or undermine disclosures fundamentally", "The misstatement results from intentional fraud by senior management", "The misstatement was discovered after the audit report was issued"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 705.06, pervasive effects are those not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items, or if so confined, represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements, or in relation to disclosures, are fundamental to users'' understanding. This drives the distinction between qualified and adverse opinions. Choice A is incorrect because pervasiveness relates to the breadth of impact across the financial statements, not simply whether the amount exceeds materiality.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Auditor's accumulation obligation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the auditor''s obligation regarding misstatements identified during the audit under AU-C 450?', '["Accumulate all identified misstatements other than those that are clearly trivial", "Report all misstatements immediately to those charged with governance", "Correct all misstatements in the working papers regardless of materiality", "Accumulate only misstatements that individually exceed performance materiality"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.04, the auditor is required to accumulate all misstatements identified during the audit, other than those that are clearly trivial. This accumulation enables the auditor to evaluate their aggregate effect near audit completion. Choice D is incorrect because the accumulation threshold is ''clearly trivial,'' which is well below performance materiality—the auditor must track even small misstatements exceeding this low threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Systematic directional bias in uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Copper Ridge Financial Group, the auditor identifies 14 uncorrected misstatements totaling $62,000—all of which overstate net income. Overall materiality is $200,000 and performance materiality is $150,000. Although the aggregate is below both thresholds, the auditor notes that every misstatement has the same directional effect. The auditor''s most appropriate response is to:', '["Accept the misstatements because the aggregate is below performance materiality", "Request that management correct at least half of the misstatements to eliminate the pattern", "Evaluate whether the directional pattern indicates possible management bias and reconsider whether the financial statements are materially misstated", "Issue a qualified opinion due to the number of misstatements identified"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor considers whether uncorrected misstatements have a pattern—such as all being in the same direction—that may indicate management bias. Even when the aggregate is below materiality, a consistent directional pattern warrants professional skepticism and reassessment of fraud risk. Choice A is incorrect because being below materiality does not eliminate concerns about bias when all misstatements systematically inflate income.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Timing of written communication of significant deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After completing the audit of Lakewood Medical Center on March 15, 2026, the engagement partner prepares the written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses. Under AU-C 265, this written communication should be made:', '["Within 30 calendar days of discovering the first control deficiency", "Before issuing the audit report so management can correct the deficiencies", "Only if management specifically requests such a communication in writing", "No later than 60 days following the report release date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor''s written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses to those charged with governance should be made no later than 60 days following the report release date. This timeline allows the auditor to complete the full evaluation. Choice B is incorrect because while the auditor may communicate orally during the audit, the formal written communication deadline is tied to the report release date, not a pre-issuance requirement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Qualitative nature of a misstatement affecting evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Thornberry Savings Bank, the auditor discovers that management intentionally omitted a $45,000 related-party disclosure. Overall materiality is $300,000. When evaluating this misstatement, the auditor should give particular consideration to:', '["Only the dollar amount of the omitted disclosure compared to materiality", "The nature and circumstances of the misstatement, including whether it involves fraud or relates to items with regulatory significance", "Whether the omission affected cash flows reported in the statement of cash flows", "The number of related-party transactions that were properly disclosed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.11(a), the auditor considers the size, nature, and particular circumstances of misstatements when evaluating their materiality. Intentional omission of related-party disclosures raises concerns about fraud and may have regulatory implications making the misstatement qualitatively material. Choice A is incorrect because evaluating only the dollar amount ignores qualitative factors such as intentional omission and the sensitivity of related-party transactions to financial statement users.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Attachment to management representation letter
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What must the auditor attach to or include with the management representation letter regarding uncorrected misstatements?', '["A copy of the engagement letter listing materiality thresholds", "The completed internal control deficiency communication", "A summary of the uncorrected misstatements identified during the audit", "A schedule of all adjusting entries proposed and accepted by management"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor includes or attaches a summary of uncorrected misstatements to the management representation letter. This summary accompanies management''s representation that it believes the effects of these items are immaterial. Choice D is incorrect because accepted adjusting entries have already been corrected in the financial statements and are not part of the uncorrected misstatement summary.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Communication options for deficiencies below significant level
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What are the auditor''s options for communicating control deficiencies that do not rise to the level of significant deficiencies or material weaknesses?', '["The auditor may communicate such deficiencies to management orally or in writing and must document any oral communication", "The auditor is required to issue a formal written report for all identified control deficiencies", "The auditor has no obligation to communicate any deficiency below the significant deficiency level", "The auditor must communicate all deficiencies directly to the audit committee regardless of severity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A19–A21, the auditor may communicate other control deficiencies to management that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. This communication may be oral or written; if oral, the auditor documents it in the workpapers. Choice C is incorrect because while not required, professional standards encourage communication of other deficiencies meriting management''s attention, and the auditor must document any oral communication made.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Design vs operating deficiency in a scenario
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Sterling Aerospace has a policy requiring dual authorization for vendor payments over $50,000. The auditor discovers that while the authorization form exists and is properly designed, the accounts payable supervisor has been routinely approving payments alone by signing both authorization lines. This situation represents:', '["A control environment weakness but not a control deficiency under AU-C 265", "A design deficiency because the authorization form should require three signatures", "A compliance violation that must be reported to regulatory authorities immediately", "An operating deficiency because a properly designed control is not operating as intended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.07, an operating deficiency exists when a control that is properly designed does not operate effectively—here, the dual-authorization control is well designed but circumvented by a single person signing both lines. The design requiring two signatures is appropriate; the failure is in execution. Choice B is incorrect because the form''s design requiring dual authorization is adequate—the deficiency lies in the supervisor bypassing the intended operation of the control.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Carryover effect of prior-year uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Meridian Software Corp., the prior-year audit identified a $30,000 overstatement of prepaid expenses that management chose not to correct. In the current year, this misstatement causes an understatement of current-year expenses by $30,000 as the prepaid balance reverses. When evaluating current-year misstatements, the auditor should:', '["Exclude the prior-year misstatement because it was already evaluated and communicated", "Only include the prior-year misstatement if management signed a new representation letter", "Include the effect of the prior-year uncorrected misstatement on the current-year financial statements", "Issue a disclaimer of opinion because prior-year misstatements were not corrected"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A7, the auditor considers the effects of uncorrected misstatements from prior periods on the current period''s financial statements. A prior-year overstatement of prepaid expenses creates a current-year understatement of expenses as the balance reverses. Choice A is incorrect because prior-year uncorrected misstatements have carryover effects that must be included in the current-year aggregate evaluation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Reassessing materiality when misstatements accumulate
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Phoenix Distribution, the auditor initially set overall materiality at $250,000. Midway through fieldwork, accumulated misstatements total $190,000, and additional testing areas remain. Under AU-C 320, the auditor''s most appropriate action is to:', '["Issue a qualified opinion immediately because misstatements are close to materiality", "Reassess whether the established materiality level remains appropriate given the volume of misstatements found", "Increase materiality to $400,000 to ensure the misstatements remain below the threshold", "Halt the audit until management corrects all currently identified misstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 320.12–.13, the auditor revises materiality for the financial statements as a whole if information comes to the auditor''s attention during the audit that would have caused a different amount to be established initially. A high volume of misstatements may signal greater risk requiring revised materiality and additional procedures. Choice C is incorrect because raising materiality solely to accommodate discovered misstatements undermines the purpose of the threshold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Management bias indicators in adjustments
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Cascade Energy observes that management consistently proposes adjusting entries near year-end that increase reported earnings, while declining to record audit-identified adjustments that would decrease earnings. Under AU-C 240, this pattern is most indicative of:', '["Possible management bias that may create a risk of material misstatement due to fraud", "Normal business operations where management exercises legitimate accounting judgment", "A significant deficiency in the revenue recognition process", "A scope limitation requiring withdrawal from the engagement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 240.A53 and AU-C 450.A18, a pattern of management adjustments that consistently increase earnings while declining income-reducing corrections is an indicator of possible management bias. The auditor should evaluate whether such bias creates a risk of material misstatement due to fraud. Choice B is incorrect because a consistent one-directional pattern goes beyond legitimate judgment and suggests intentional bias in financial reporting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Evaluating misstatements with offsetting effects on different line items
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northgate Properties, the auditor identifies an uncorrected $180,000 overstatement of rental revenue and an uncorrected $160,000 overstatement of operating expenses, both affecting the income statement. Overall materiality is $200,000. When evaluating the aggregate effect of these misstatements, the auditor should:', '["Net the misstatements to conclude the income effect is only $20,000 and therefore clearly trivial", "Evaluate only the larger misstatement since it is closer to materiality", "Report both misstatements as material since each individually approaches materiality", "Consider the effect of each misstatement on relevant line items rather than simply netting them against each other"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A11, the auditor does not simply offset misstatements affecting different line items, even when they partially cancel on net income. Each misstatement may be material to its respective financial statement line item—revenue is overstated by $180,000 and expenses by $160,000. Choice A is incorrect because netting misstatements masks their individual significance to specific financial statement elements and could mislead users relying on those line items.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — IT segregation of duties control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Horizon Data Services, the auditor discovers that the IT administrator has both the ability to initiate transactions and modify the audit log that records all system activity. Management states this is necessary due to staffing constraints. Under auditing standards, this situation represents:', '["An acceptable staffing arrangement that does not affect internal control", "A compliance deficiency requiring immediate disclosure to regulatory authorities", "A control deficiency related to inadequate segregation of duties over IT functions", "A design deficiency only if actual unauthorized transactions have been recorded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A5, inadequate segregation of duties—particularly when the same individual can both initiate transactions and alter audit trails—represents a control deficiency. The ability to modify audit logs undermines detective controls regardless of whether unauthorized transactions have occurred. Choice D is incorrect because a control deficiency exists based on the potential for misstatement, not only when an actual misstatement has been identified.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Combination of related deficiencies forming material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Pacifica Health Systems, the auditor identifies three control deficiencies: the CFO does not review journal entries over $10,000, bank reconciliations are prepared but not independently reviewed, and there is no policy for investigating reconciling items older than 30 days. Individually, none qualifies as a material weakness. The auditor should evaluate whether:', '["The combination of these deficiencies, considered together, represents a material weakness because they affect the same financial reporting objective", "Each deficiency should be reported as a separate significant deficiency without further analysis", "Only the CFO review deficiency matters because it involves the highest authority level", "The deficiencies can be ignored since none individually reaches the material weakness threshold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor considers whether individual control deficiencies that are not material weaknesses on their own may combine to create a material weakness when they share a common financial reporting objective. Here, all three affect the cash and journal entry review process. Choice D is incorrect because auditing standards explicitly require the auditor to evaluate combinations—individually insignificant deficiencies can constitute a material weakness when interrelated.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Documentation requirements for misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After completing the evaluation of uncorrected misstatements for Evergreen Manufacturing, the engagement partner prepares the audit documentation. Under AU-C 450, the auditor is required to document:', '["Only the total dollar amount of uncorrected misstatements and the materiality level", "The amount below which misstatements are regarded as clearly trivial, all misstatements accumulated during the audit, and the conclusion on whether uncorrected misstatements are material", "A signed acknowledgment from each member of the audit team who identified misstatements", "The specific audit procedures that would have been necessary to correct each misstatement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.15, the auditor documents the clearly trivial threshold, all misstatements accumulated during the audit and whether they have been corrected, and the conclusion about whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in aggregate. Choice A is incorrect because documentation must include the clearly trivial amount, each individual misstatement—not merely the total—and the basis for the auditor''s overall conclusion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Handling management's partial acceptance of proposed adjustments
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bayshore Logistics, the auditor proposes five adjusting journal entries totaling $95,000 to correct identified misstatements. Management agrees to record three entries totaling $60,000 but declines the remaining two totaling $35,000. The auditor should:', '["Refuse to issue any opinion until management records all proposed adjustments", "Treat the $35,000 as a scope limitation requiring a qualified or disclaimer of opinion", "Record the declined entries directly in the client''s general ledger", "Include the $35,000 of declined adjustments in the summary of uncorrected misstatements and evaluate their effect"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.07–.08, management may decline to record some proposed adjustments. The auditor adds declined adjustments to the summary of uncorrected misstatements and evaluates the aggregate effect on the financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because management is not required to record all proposed adjustments—the auditor evaluates whether the uncorrected amounts, individually or in aggregate, are material to the opinion.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Requesting management examine a class of transactions
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Coastal Freight Corp., the auditor identifies misstatements in freight revenue from sampling that suggest a systematic recording error. Under AU-C 450, the auditor may request management to:', '["Examine the class of transactions to identify and correct misstatements in the population beyond those found in the sample", "Immediately restate the prior three years of financial statements for freight revenue", "Hire an external specialist to recalculate all freight revenue for the period", "Waive the misstatements if the sampling confidence level was below 95 percent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.06, when the auditor identifies a misstatement, the auditor may request management to examine a class of transactions, account balance, or disclosure to identify and correct misstatements in the group. This is particularly appropriate when sampling suggests a systematic error affecting the population. Choice D is incorrect because the confidence level does not provide a basis for waiving identified misstatements—the auditor must still evaluate them.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Control environment deficiencies indicating fraud risk
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Summit Capital Advisors, the auditor discovers that the CEO personally overrides month-end closing journal entry review controls, the CFO has exclusive access to post manual journal entries without secondary approval, and reconciliation exceptions are routinely suppressed from management reports. The auditor should evaluate these findings primarily as:', '["Three separate significant deficiencies that should be communicated individually", "Operating deficiencies that are adequately mitigated by the entity''s external audit", "Indicators of a control environment that may create conditions conducive to fraud, requiring reassessment of fraud risk under AU-C 240", "Common staffing limitations that are typical for small management teams"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 240.A30 and AU-C 265.A13, management override of controls, lack of segregation of duties, and suppression of reconciliation exceptions collectively indicate a control environment facilitating fraudulent financial reporting. These conditions require the auditor to reconsider fraud risk assessments and expand procedures. Choice D is incorrect because systematic override, exclusive access, and suppression of exceptions go far beyond typical staffing constraints and signal intentional circumvention.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Three categories of misstatements in AU-C 450
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What are the three categories of misstatements described in AU-C 450?', '["Known, likely, and possible misstatements", "Factual misstatements, judgmental misstatements, and projected misstatements", "Quantitative, qualitative, and disclosure misstatements", "Immaterial, material, and pervasive misstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A1, misstatements are categorized as factual (no doubt about the error), judgmental (arising from management''s estimates or accounting policy selections the auditor considers unreasonable), and projected (extrapolated from audit sample results to the population). Choice A is incorrect because while ''known'' and ''likely'' are used informally, the authoritative categories in AU-C 450 are factual, judgmental, and projected.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Individually immaterial misstatements that are collectively material
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Westfield Consolidated, the auditor has accumulated 23 uncorrected misstatements, each between $5,000 and $15,000. None individually approaches the $200,000 overall materiality. However, the aggregate totals $215,000 and all misstatements overstate assets. Management declines to make corrections. The auditor''s most appropriate response is to:', '["Accept the financial statements because no single misstatement is material", "Communicate the misstatements to governance but issue an unmodified opinion", "Request a management representation letter and take no further action", "Determine that the uncorrected misstatements in aggregate are material and consider the effect on the auditor''s opinion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in the aggregate. When 23 misstatements total $215,000—exceeding the $200,000 materiality—and all overstate assets, the aggregate is material. If management refuses to correct, the auditor considers modifying the opinion. Choice A is incorrect because the standard requires evaluation of the aggregate effect, not just individual misstatement amounts.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Documentation of oral communication of control deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Ridgeway Construction communicated several control deficiencies orally to management during fieldwork that were below the significant deficiency threshold. Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s documentation requirement for these oral communications is:', '["The auditor must document the oral communication of other deficiencies in the audit workpapers", "No documentation is required for deficiencies communicated only orally", "The auditor must obtain management''s written acknowledgment of each oral communication", "The auditor must include all oral communications in the final written communication to governance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A22, when the auditor communicates other control deficiencies orally to management, the auditor documents the communication in the audit workpapers. This ensures a record exists even for deficiencies below the significant deficiency threshold. Choice B is incorrect because auditing standards require documentation of oral communications about control deficiencies to maintain an adequate audit trail.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Identified material weakness requiring revision of audit strategy
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During interim testing at Crestline Insurance, the auditor identifies a material weakness in the claims reserving process: actuarial assumptions are not reviewed by anyone independent of the person preparing them. Year-end testing has not yet begun. The auditor discovers this deficiency in October for a December 31 fiscal year-end. The auditor should:', '["Withdraw from the engagement because a material weakness cannot be remediated during the audit", "Revise the audit strategy to increase the nature, timing, and extent of substantive procedures over claims reserves in light of the heightened risk", "Accept the client''s oral promise to implement a review procedure before year-end as sufficient mitigation", "Defer evaluation of the deficiency until after year-end procedures are completed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A8 and AU-C 315.31, identifying a material weakness requires the auditor to reconsider assessed risks and revise the planned audit approach, including enhancing substantive procedures over the affected area. Discovering this at interim allows time to redesign the year-end strategy. Choice C is incorrect because an oral promise to implement a control does not reduce audit risk—the auditor must rely on tested, operating controls or expanded substantive procedures.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
